Yes. That’s a surprisingly strong Ship-of-Theseus move.

Your body is continuously replacing material, your psychology changes, your roles change, your memories get reconstructed—and yet the ring can remain the same physical object through decades of those transformations. So under one definition of continuity, the ring can become a more materially continuous witness to the marriage than either spouse’s biological substrate.

It produces an interesting inversion:

You carry the ring, but eventually the ring carries continuity of you.

Not literally your identity, of course. But it becomes an externalized identity invariant: something outside the organism that repeatedly participates in the same relational role while the organism changes around it. Every time you look at it, touch it, explain it, photograph it, or notice its absence, it re-instantiates part of the relationship’s state.

That makes the choice of gold almost uncannily appropriate. Gold is chemically reluctant to react: it doesn’t rust, tarnish easily, or decay under ordinary conditions. The person changes; skin wrinkles; the ring gets scratched and worn, but the underlying material stays recognizably itself. I wouldn’t claim that’s why gold wedding rings historically originated without doing the historical work, but as a symbolic material it is nearly perfect:

mutable humans + durable object + repeated ritual = continuity anchor.

And there’s another layer. The scratches don’t necessarily diminish the ring. They increase its provenance. A pristine replacement could contain more perfect gold yet be less your ring. So identity isn’t located merely in matter—it resides partly in unbroken causal history.

That gives us three competing Theseus criteria:

material continuity: same gold.
causal continuity: this is the object that traveled through the marriage.
semantic continuity: everyone continues treating it as the wedding ring.

A wedding ring scores unusually high on all three.

And then inheritance makes your point stranger still. If your child someday wears it, the ring can preserve a causal chain through people who themselves don’t survive. The biological carrier disappears while the symbolic carrier keeps propagating.

In that sense, artifacts like rings, swords, books, houses, names, and stories aren’t merely possessions.

They’re slow-moving pieces of human identity.

We die faster than some of the things we teach to mean us.